  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the chainring?",
      "a": "The chainring is the toothed gear attached to the pedals. Its teeth pull the chain along as you pedal. Many bikes have two or three chainrings of different sizes.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the front gear on a bike?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is a low gear easier for going uphill?",
      "a": "In a low gear the chain runs on a big rear sprocket, so the wheel turns fewer times per pedal turn. You trade speed for force, so each push on the pedals needs less effort.",
      "asks": [
        "Which gear should I use uphill?",
        "Why does a big back gear help?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the chain do?",
      "a": "The chain is a loop of linked rollers that wraps around the chainring and the sprocket. It carries the pull from the front gear to the back gear without slipping.",
      "asks": [
        "How does the chain move the wheel?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the derailleur do?",
      "a": "The derailleur is a spring-loaded arm near the rear sprockets. When you shift, a cable moves it sideways, guiding the chain onto a bigger or smaller sprocket, and it keeps the chain tight.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a bike change gear?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do you work out a bike's gear ratio?",
      "a": "Divide the chainring teeth by the sprocket teeth. A 44-tooth chainring with an 11-tooth sprocket gives 4, so the wheel turns four times per pedal turn.",
      "asks": [
        "How many times does the wheel turn per pedal turn?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The number of back-wheel turns per pedal turn equals chainring teeth divided by rear sprocket teeth.",
    "Derailleur gears became common on racing bikes in the 1930s and 1950s; the Tour de France allowed them from 1937."
  ],
